---
// The explanation below the Lab: the data, the symbols, how a tree is grown,
// choosing the depth, and the model in short. Static HTML; formulas are
// rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="tree-data">
    <h2 id="tree-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      Each shape is a day at the seaside. Across the graph is the temperature;
      up the graph is the wind. Circles were good beach days and triangles were
      days to stay home. Good days are warm and not too windy, but a few days
      break the rule, as real days do.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lines cut the chart into rectangles. Each rectangle is a leaf of the
      tree below it, and its tint shows the tree's answer for any day that lands
      there. Days the tree gets wrong have a dark ring. The numbers are made up,
      and <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another 20 days.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tree-symbols">
    <h2 id="tree-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>node</dt>
        <dd>
          A group of days, and the question that splits it in two. The top node
          holds every day.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>leaf</dt>
        <dd>A node with no question: it gives the answer.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>pₖ</dt>
        <dd>The share of days in a node that are of kind k.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">G</dt>
        <dd>
          Gini impurity: how mixed a node is. 0 when every day is the same kind;
          0.5 when two kinds are split half and half.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>gain</dt>
        <dd>How much a question lowers the impurity.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-a">d</dt>
        <dd>The depth: how many questions in a row the tree may ask.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tree-how">
    <h2 id="tree-how">How a tree is grown</h2>
    <p>
      A decision tree is a list of yes-or-no questions, like "Is it 19 °C or
      colder?" Each answer sends a day left (yes) or right (no), until it
      reaches a leaf. The tree is grown from the top, one node at a time:
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Measure how mixed the node is.</strong>
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.gini} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Try every question:</strong> each feature, and every threshold
        halfway between two neighbouring values. Score each by how much purer
        its two halves are.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.gain} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep the question with the highest gain</strong>, and grow each
        half the same way.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stop</strong> when a node holds only one kind, or the tree
        reaches its depth limit. A leaf answers with the most common kind among
        its days.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The tree is greedy: it picks the best question for each node on its own,
      without looking ahead. Move a split line and the gain drops, because the
      tree had already found the best place. The questions below it are then
      grown again from the new halves.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tree-depth">
    <h2 id="tree-depth">Choosing the depth</h2>
    <p>
      A shallow tree asks few questions, so it can only draw a few straight
      borders. Two questions are enough here: "Is it warm?" then "Is it calm?"
    </p>
    <p>
      A deep tree keeps asking until almost every leaf is pure, so it gets
      nearly all of its own days right. But it does that by drawing small boxes
      around the odd days, and a new day that lands in one of those boxes gets a
      strange answer. The score <strong>Right on its own data</strong> always
      rises with depth, so it cannot tell you when to stop. In practice, the
      depth is chosen by testing the tree on days it has not seen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tree-summary">
    <h2 id="tree-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Ask</strong> the yes-or-no question that makes the two halves
        purest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Repeat</strong> in each half.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stop</strong> at pure nodes or at the depth limit; each leaf
        answers with its most common kind.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Predict</strong> by following the questions from the top down to
        a leaf.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
